WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.910
JENNY BROWN: Welcome back
to "Cloud Storage Bytes."

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.920
So far, we've talked
a lot about how

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.860
to use Google Cloud
Storage, from managing data

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.900
to optimizing performance.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.620
But there is one important topic
that we haven't discussed yet,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.640
the price tag.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.710
Have you ever gone grocery
shopping while you're hungry

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.860
and you ended up blowing your
budget right out of the water?

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.530
I find that a list can be
really helpful for me here

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.770
so that I don't overestimate
my snack requirements.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.130
This kind of planning can be
helpful with cloud storage too.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.760
Just like going
grocery shopping,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.610
it's a good idea to
predict and track

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.150
your data usage so you can
anticipate your monthly costs.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.060
Since Cloud Storage Billing is
a little bit more complicated

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.000
than grocery shopping, we'll
need to take a closer look.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.120
Pricing is a compilation of
several different components--

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.990
data storage, networking,
operations, and retrieval

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.090
and early deletion fees.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.700
Each of these components
has its own pricing tables

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.180
that show costs based on factors
such as region and operation

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.200
type.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.170
This means that each
company's total cost

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:07.740
is going to be
completely different

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.320
and based upon
specific requirements.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.790
So as much as I'd like to tell
you exactly what your bottom

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.860
line is going to be, I can't.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.360
Instead, I can give an overview
of each pricing category

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.660
using my company, Jen's Jams.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.820
Suppose I have the
following storage usage

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.680
pattern in a given month.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.410
Let's find out how much
my company will pay.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:34.130
The first two lines here are
in the data storage category.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.090
Data storage costs apply to your
at-rest storage of your data

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.680
in cloud storage.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.750
For a quick refresher,
at rest means

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.110
that the data is physically
on the disk itself and not

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.210
somewhere else in transit
through the network

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.850
or only temporarily
housed there.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.920
Regional or
multi-regional storage

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.430
is appropriate for storing data
that's frequently accessed,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.540
such as serving website content,
interactive workloads, or data

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.240
supporting mobile and
gaming applications.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.880
For Jen's Jams, we've picked
multi-regional storage

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.389
because we've got customers
all over the world.

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:11.030
My company's costs for
multi-region storage

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.440
is $0.026 per gigabyte.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.680
So the total cost for
multi-regional storage

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.860
this month is going
to be $1,597.44.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.840
The majority of
my company's music

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.030
is held in the
multi-regional storage

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.300
so that each song can be
as accessible as possible.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.720
The next expense is
also for data storage,

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.270
but this is for
nearline storage.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.130
Nearline storage,
as you'd remember,

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.070
is a low-cost, highly
durable storage service

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.340
for storing infrequently
accessed data.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.620
It's a better choice than
multi-regional storage

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.230
or regional storage
in scenarios where

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.590
slightly lower availability,
30-day minimum storage

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.690
duration, and costs
for data access

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.130
are acceptable trade-offs
for lower storage costs.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.590
Nearline storage is ideal for
infrequently read or modified

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:01.310
data.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.440
We're talking once
a month or less.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.590
And nearline storage is also
appropriate for data backup,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.620
disaster recovery,
and archival storage.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.870
At Jen's Jams, we
use nearline storage

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:14.060
to backup our music
collection in the event

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.800
that a song gets lost.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.020
I also use nearline
storage for songs that

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.690
are rarely ever downloaded.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.810
Nearline storage for the
US multi-region location

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.890
is $0.01 per gigabyte.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:30.170
So the total cost
here is $1,024.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.510
Notice that this is
less than the total cost

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:37.130
of multi-regional storage, even
though I'm storing more data.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.250
Next up in my pricing
table is network costs.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.490
Network usage charges apply
when an object or object

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.690
metadata is read from your
buckets, that is, egress.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:49.850
The cost depends
on several factors

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.760
and includes
caveats, for example,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.360
circumstances with the
network egress within GCP.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.940
For example, data
egress from your bucket

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.850
to a non-cloud storage
Google Cloud Platform service

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:05.390
is free if your bucket
is located in a region,

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:08.510
the GCP service is
located in a multi-region,

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.210
and both locations are
on the same continent.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:15.590
So accessing data in a US East
1 bucket with a US app engine

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.269
instance would be free.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:20.250
My company's egress falls
under general network usage,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.760
so none of those
special use cases apply.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.470
Network ingress is free.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:28.130
An operation is an action that
makes changes to or retrieves

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:32.330
information about buckets
and objects in cloud storage.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.840
Operations are divided into
three different categories,

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.230
class A, class B, and free.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.900
There's a lot to each of these
categories, so, for details,

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.480
go ahead and check the
linked documentation.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.420
Costs for operations
vary by operation class

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.190
and storage class.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.240
In my case, class A operations
are in multi-region storage,

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:57.140
so the cost is $0.05
per 10,000 operations.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.990
There are class B operations
in multi-regional storage

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.510
data and nearline
storage data, and those

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:03.930
are priced differently.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.830
My totals are $4.00 and
$1.00, respectively.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.490
Because nearline storage
and coldline storage

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.590
are intended for storing
infrequently accessed data,

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.520
there are additional costs
associated with retrieval

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:18.580
and minimum storage durations.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.590
But again, more about
that in the documentation.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:22.780
And there you have it.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.210
My company's cloud
storage usage comes out

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:29.902
to a total of $7,460.22.

00:05:29.902 --> 00:05:31.360
There's quite a
bit more to billing

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.800
than what I've shown you today.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.860
And prices may change after
this video is released.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.440
So please be sure to check
out the documentation linked

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.650
below for the most up-to-date
pricing information.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:43.750
There's plenty more to
discuss about billing,

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.000
but that's a totally
different episode.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.262
Don't forget to
subscribe, give us a like,

00:05:48.262 --> 00:05:50.470
and tell us what you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:51.430
Storage.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:54.620
Thanks for joining us on this
quick byte of cloud storage.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.390
See you next time.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

